  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   We improve upon the provided code by introducing a **multi-resolution optimization with gradient-based refinement** and **kernel-based initialization**. The core idea is to:  \n   - Start with a high-quality initialization derived from a kernel density across multiple scales.  \n   - Use adaptive gradient descent to minimize the maximum overlap, leveraging analytical gradients.  \n   - Combine results across resolutions (200-400 points) to escape local optima.  \n   - Replace stochastic local search with a gradient-driven method that focuses on high-influence lags to speed up convergence.  \n   This approach scales better than brute-force correlation and explores higher resolutions (up to 400 points) while respecting the time budget.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   The objective is:  \n   \\[\\min_C \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))\\,dx\\]  \n   where \\(C_5 = \\frac{\\max_k C}{\\Delta x}\\), \\(\\Delta x = 2/N\\), and \\(h:[0,2]\\to[0,1]\\) satisfies \\(\\int_0^2 h(x)\\,dx=1\\).  \n\n   We approximate \\(h\\) as a piecewise function on a grid, grouped by influence intervals. The analytical gradient of the overlap is:  \n   \\[\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial h_i} = \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{T}} \\Delta x \\cdot \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial h_i} [h_j(1-h_{j+k})]\\]  \n   where \\(\\mathcal{T}\\) is a subset of lag indices determined by dynamic influence propagation.  \n\n   Constraints:  \n   - Bounded values: \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\)  \n   - Integral constraint: \\(\\sum h_i = N/2\\)  \n\n   **Solver**:  \n   - Stage 1: Gradient descent using line search with adaptive lag selection.  \n   - Stage 2: Second-order refinement with projected gradients.  \n\n   **Resolution handling**:  \n   Solutions adapt to \\(N \\in \\{200, 250, 300, 350, 400\\}\\) with smooth interpolation and projection.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   a. Initialization: Create a \"kernel transfer\" function from the parent using:  \n      \\[\\phi(t) = \\exp\\left(-\\frac{t^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)\\]  \n      clipped to \\([0,1]\\) scaled to unit area.  \n\n   b. Optimization loop:  \n      i. Compute gradients using top-k lag selection with:  \n         \\[L = \\{(k, \\|\\nabla C_k\\|)\\}_{k=-N+1}^{N-1}\\]  \n         select top 5% by gradient magnitude.  \n      ii. Perform line search:  \n          \\[h_{\\text{trial}} = h - \\alpha \\frac{1}{\\|\\nabla C\\|} \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{T}} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial h_i} [h_j(1-h_{j+k})]\\]  \n      iii. Project to constraints using dual projection:  \n          \\[\\begin{cases} h_i = \\min(\\max(h_i, 0), 1) \\\\ \\sum h_i = N/2 \\end{cases}\\]  \n\n   c. Adaptation to new \\(N\\):  \n      - Interpolate parent solution to target resolution  \n      - Re-evaluate integral with gradient correction  \n      - Reproject if necessary  \n\n4. Search choices and variations  \n   - **Resolution transfer**:  \n     Solutions at \\(N=200,250,300,350,400\\) are dynamically transferred.  \n     Wythoff's array sequence generates fallback points:  \n     \\[s_j = \\lfloor j \\cdot \\phi \\rfloor, \\quad \\phi = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{2}\\]  \n\n   - **Gradient stabilization**:  \n     Clip gradients at \\(10^3\\) to avoid overflow; use lazy projection with Lagrangian multipliers for integral constraints.  \n\n5. Budget and robustness  \n   - Each descent step: 0.5s at \\(N=400\\)  \n   - Early termination if correlation exceeds historical \\(\\max_{5}\\) steps  \n   - Fallback: Linear interpolation w/ gradient projection  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   - Final \\(h\\) is projected and evaluated for:  \n     1. All \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\)  \n     2. \\(\\sum h_i = N/2\\) with tolerance \\(10^{-6}\\)  \n   - Return `h`, `c5_value`, `N` with `compute_c5` reused for validation.  \n   - Score improvement threshold: \\(\\Delta C > -5\\%\\) trigger refinement.",
